    "title": "Write Your Own Two-Chord Vamp",
    "summary": "Three steps and one check build a modal vamp on any root, and the check is that the loop has to refuse to arrive anywhere.",
    "tags": ["modes", "theory", "ear"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every vamp in this pathway was built to one rule, and "
        },
        {
          "text": "What the Second Chord Is For",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-the-vamp-rule"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " is where it got stated. Turning it into something you can run on any root takes three steps."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "text": "Pick home — a chord you can actually stand on, major or minor, with a perfect fifth in it. That choice alone puts you in one of the two families."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "Pick the note — the single tone you want to lean on. That choice is the mode."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "Find the chord of that mode which contains the note, and make it the second chord of the loop."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every mode in this pathway has lived on "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — comparing seven modes note against note only works from one root. A recipe only earns the name if it runs somewhere besides the root it was built on, so here it goes on four others."
        }
      ]
    },
