    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The first of those four is a loop you have already heard. "
        },
        {
          "text": "When the Progression Starts to Function",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-when-a-progression-functions"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " built "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " from scratch, played it with nowhere confirmed as home, and only settled it into "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " major once a third chord arrived on the end."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Before you commit to a pair of chords, run one check that has nothing to do with which note you're after: does the loop actually circle, or does it quietly arrive somewhere? Every major triad is the dominant of the triad a perfect fourth above it — a "
        },
        {
          "text": "V",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " looking for its "
        },
        {
          "text": "I",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — so "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " all settle on that second chord instead of circling back — you've written a cadence with the chords in the wrong order, not a vamp. That's exactly why neither major-family vamp on "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " used the fourth: "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " goes a whole tone up and "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a whole tone down, because "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the dominant of neither."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A minor "
        },
        {
          "text": "i",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "IV",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " doesn't have this problem — "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " isn't the dominant of "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", which is the whole reason "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " works as Dorian's own vamp instead of arriving anywhere. "
        },
        {
          "text": "When the Progression Starts to Function",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-when-a-progression-functions"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " is where arriving got its own definition, if the distinction still feels abstract."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "There's a second, plainer check: the second chord has to be inside the mode. A chord carrying a note the mode doesn't have hasn't coloured the loop — it's changed the note set underneath it entirely."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "None of this makes the second chord the only one that would work. In A Dorian, three of the seven diatonic triads hold the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " — "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#dim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — and "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the one this pathway keeps reaching for because it's the plainest chord a hand can hold down for a whole bar, not because the other two don't exist. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dorian: Minor Without the Ache",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-dorian-sound"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already showed "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " turning into "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " by raising one note; which of those three to build a vamp on is a separate decision from finding the note itself."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A two-chord loop is as far as any of this goes — building it into a longer progression, or a whole song, is a different craft with its own rules. One more thing worth naming before you close this out: borrowing a chord from a parallel mode into a key that's still functioning is called modal interchange, and it's the natural next thing once a vamp like this one feels obvious."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Pick your own root, run the three steps, and go find out whether it actually circles. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Chord Shapes",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/chord-shapes"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " has whichever two chords you land on, and a slow, even click from "
        },
        {
          "text": "Metronome",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/metronome"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " is enough to tell a real vamp from one that's already halfway to arriving."
        }
      ]
    }
